Statement
----------

Let V be a d-dimensional vector space over a field k of characteristic
different from two, with a nondegenerate symmetric bilinear form B. Let
H be a subgroup of the isometry group of B. Suppose k[H] contains the orthogonal projector p onto a
nondegenerate line L=k*v. If span(v*H)=V, then

                         k[H]=End_k(V).

Here vectors act on the right, and k[H] means the unital matrix algebra
spanned by H. In the acquired rank-four setting d=7, k=F11, and

                         p=(I+a)(I+e)/4.

The commuting involutory isometries a,e have the acquired common positive
line L, with joint dimensions1,2,2,2. Thus p has rank one and is self-adjoint,
its image is L, and its kernel is L-perp. The stored seed has nonzero norm,
so B(v,v) is nonzero. These are the exact endpoint/form premises from the
preceding argument, not inferences from a group label.

Proof and right-action order
-----------------------------

Choose h_0,...,h_(d-1) in H so that w_i=v*h_i form a basis of V. The
orthogonal projection formula is

                  x*p = B(x,v)/B(v,v) * v.

For each ordered pair(i,j), put E_ij=h_i^-1*p*h_j. Since all three factors
belong to k[H], so does E_ij. Orthogonality gives

 B(x*h_i^-1,v)=B(x,v*h_i)=B(x,w_i).

Consequently the actual RIGHT_ROW order is

       x*(h_i^-1*p*h_j) = B(x,w_i)/B(v,v) * w_j.       (1)

The inverse is on h_i before p. Reversing these factors would not give
the displayed functional. There is no transpose or word reversal hidden
in(1): it follows from the stated right action and B-invariance.

Nondegeneracy of B makes the map w -> B(-,w) an isomorphism V -> V*.
Since the w_i form a basis and B(v,v)!=0, the d functionals

                    alpha_i=B(-,w_i)/B(v,v)

form a basis of V*. Equation(1) says E_ij is the rank-one operator
x -> alpha_i(x)*w_j. These d^2 operators are linearly independent: if
sum(c_ij E_ij)=0, then for every j the w_j coefficient is the zero
functional sum_i(c_ij alpha_i), forcing every c_ij=0. Their number is
dim_k End_k(V)=d^2. They therefore form a basis of End_k(V) contained in
k[H], proving the assertion.

The E_ij need not themselves be matrix units relative to the w_i, because
the alpha_i need not be the dual basis of the w_i. They are a basis of
rank-one operators, which is exactly what the argument needs. No Burnside
theorem, algebraic closure, semisimplicity or subgroup classification is used.

For an involutory generating quartet, each h_i is a word in the original
